  <static seed="306" source="Library/Rochester/setDiffEQ5ModelingWith1stOrder/ns7_4_31a.pg">
    <statement><p>A tank contains <m>2780</m> L of  pure water.  A solution
that contains <m>0.07</m> kg of sugar per liter enters the 
tank at the rate <m>4</m> L/min. The solution is mixed and drains
from the tank at the same rate.</p>
<p>(a) How much sugar is in the tank at the beginning?</p>
<p><m>y(0) =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0001"/> (include units)</p>
<p>(b) With <m>S</m> representing the amount of sugar (in kg) at time t (in minutes) write a
differential equation which models this situation.</p>
<p><m>S' = f(t,S) =</m>  <var height="3" name="AnSwEr0002" width="60"/>.</p>
<p><alert> Note: </alert> Make sure you use a capital S, (and don't use S(t), it confuses the computer).
Don't enter units for this function.</p>
<p>(c) Find the amount of sugar (in kg) after t  minutes.</p>
<p><m>S(t) =</m> <fillin characters="40" name="AnSwEr0003"/>  (function of t)</p>
<p>(d) Find the amount of the sugar after 54 minutes.</p>
<p><m>S(54)=</m> <fillin characters="40" name="AnSwEr0004"/> (include <url href=""/>)</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0\ {\rm kg}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0.28-4\frac{S}{2780}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>194.6\mathopen{}\left(1-e^{-0.0014388t}\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>14.548\ {\rm kg}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </static>
